<h1>Ecosystems</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">{{ versions.vexes.ecosystems }} supported ecosystems, {{ versions.vexes.lockfiles }} lockfile formats, auto-detection, and ecosystem-specific behavior.</p>

    <h2 id="supported-ecosystems">Supported Ecosystems</h2>

    <p>vexes auto-detects lockfiles in the current directory and its subdirectories. No configuration needed -- it finds and parses whatever lockfiles exist in your project.</p>

    <table class="cmd-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Ecosystem</th><th>Lockfiles</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>npm</td><td><code>package-lock.json</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>pnpm</td><td><code>pnpm-lock.yaml</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Yarn</td><td><code>yarn.lock</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>PyPI</td><td><code>poetry.lock</code>, <code>Pipfile.lock</code>, <code>requirements.txt</code>, <code>pyproject.toml</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cargo</td><td><code>Cargo.lock</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Go</td><td><code>go.sum</code>, <code>go.mod</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>RubyGems</td><td><code>Gemfile.lock</code>, <code>Gemfile</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Composer</td><td><code>composer.lock</code>, <code>composer.json</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>NuGet</td><td><code>packages.lock.json</code>, <code>*.csproj</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Maven / Gradle</td><td><code>gradle.lockfile</code>, <code>pom.xml</code></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 id="auto-detection">Auto-Detection</h2>

    <p>When you run <code>vexes scan</code> or <code>vexes analyze</code> without specifying an ecosystem, vexes walks the directory tree looking for any recognized lockfile. If your project has both <code>package-lock.json</code> and <code>Cargo.lock</code>, both ecosystems are scanned in a single run.</p>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">vexes scan</span>

<span class="dim">  Found 3 lockfile(s):
    package-lock.json    (npm)    — 312 packages
    Cargo.lock           (cargo)  — 147 packages
    poetry.lock          (pypi)   — 89 packages

  Scanning 548 packages across 3 ecosystems...</span></div>

    <h2 id="filtering">Ecosystem Filtering</h2>

    <p>Use <code>--ecosystem</code> to scan only one ecosystem. This is useful in monorepos where you want to run separate CI jobs per ecosystem.</p>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">vexes scan --ecosystem npm</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">vexes analyze --ecosystem cargo</span></div>

    <p>The ecosystem name is case-insensitive. All of the following are equivalent: <code>npm</code>, <code>NPM</code>, <code>Npm</code>.</p>

    <h2 id="ecosystem-details">Ecosystem-Specific Behavior</h2>

    <h3>npm / pnpm / Yarn</h3>
    <p>Full support for all four detection layers. The npm registry provides the richest metadata (publish history, maintainer accounts, download counts), making Layer 4 analysis most effective here. Deep analysis (Layer 1) downloads tarballs from the npm registry.</p>

    <h3>PyPI</h3>
    <p>Full vulnerability scanning via OSV. Behavioral analysis covers dependency graph profiling and registry metadata. Deep analysis downloads wheels/sdists and runs Python AST inspection.</p>

    <h3>Cargo</h3>
    <p>Full vulnerability scanning via OSV (RustSec advisory database). Behavioral analysis covers dependency graphs and crates.io metadata.</p>

    <h3>Go</h3>
    <p>Vulnerability scanning via OSV (Go vulnerability database). Dependency graph analysis from <code>go.sum</code> and <code>go.mod</code>.</p>

    <h3>RubyGems, Composer, NuGet, Maven/Gradle</h3>
    <p>Vulnerability scanning via OSV. Dependency graph profiling (Layers 2-3). Registry metadata analysis varies by ecosystem -- some registries expose more publish history than others.</p>

    <h2 id="lockfile-priority">Lockfile Priority</h2>

    <p>When multiple lockfiles exist for the same ecosystem (e.g., both <code>package-lock.json</code> and <code>yarn.lock</code>), vexes uses the most recently modified lockfile. This avoids double-counting packages in projects that have switched package managers but not cleaned up old lockfiles.</p>

    <h2 id="adding-ecosystems">Adding Ecosystem Support</h2>

    <p>vexes is designed with a parser interface that makes adding new ecosystems straightforward. Each ecosystem parser implements lockfile detection, package extraction, and registry API integration. Contributions for new ecosystems are welcome via pull request.</p>
